    "title": "Split-Zero Geometry and Common Deformation Registers: Project Atlas, Exact Results, Formalization, and Visualizations",
    "doi": "10.5281/zenodo.21426216",
    "publication_date": "2026-07-18",
    "description": "<p>This is the human-readable public home for a continuing mathematics project connecting explicit Erdos--Strauss arithmetic registers, split-zero and tagged-sedenion models, finite moonshine and Sigma(2,3,5) calculations, exact formal checks, and selected geometric experiments. The Project Atlas separates exact theorems, checked calculations, generated constructions, obstructions, diagnostics, and genuinely open interfaces.</p><p>The retained payload includes the Project Atlas, bookmarked result-packet compendium, Lean and Python sources, proof-status and axiom audits, machine-readable ledgers, editable working texts, visual sources, scene data, replay material, and the Part 8-C2B residual-Niemeier audit.</p><p><strong>New bounded supplement:</strong> <code>07_Predatum_K4_Hopf_WorkingNotes_and_Checks_20260718.zip</code> preserves the N16--N18 sequence on the K4 sphere, the terminology/typing charter, and the Hopf-fibration section-sheaf candidate, together with the cumulative lane ledger, executable scripts, recorded outputs, a fresh UTF-8 rerun, manifest, and hashes. The four script groups reran at 12/12, 17/17, 10/10, and 12/12 encoded checks. N17 explicitly contributes no new theorem; it fixes terminology and dependency direction.</p><p><strong>Status.</strong> This is a working research record, not peer review, not a completed universal framework, and not a proof of a famous open problem. The script reruns certify only their encoded finite symbolic checks. Topos-level statements, bundle classification, cited topology, and the referee-recorded Gauss-linking computation remain identified external or non-machine-checked steps. The Project Atlas PDF remains the recommended entry point.</p>",
